[{:seon.task/name "a-red-in-the-daily-full-test-is-a-task"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: yes, and \"how do we make sure we don't keep creating new tasks?\" What exists: the daily bin/test --all on main answers seon.prove/full-answered, which raises one :platform problem, :seon.prove/full-run-red, naming every red deftest, and that problem never closes as the reds are fixed one by one. Correct: each red deftest is one held task whose identity is the deftest's name (a task named from the var, as the failing deftest seon.agent-test/foo gives one task entity), written by upsert, so the next day's red on the same deftest is the same entity, never a new one; a green of that deftest in a later full test closes it, and the one problem goes. No hash: the var's name already identifies it. Proofs, red first: two full answers with the same red deftest leave one task; a green answer after it closes that task; two different reds make two. See docs/sharing.md#a-result-stands-until-what-it-read-changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/two-full-answers-name-same-red-deftest-leave}} {:seon.task/name "the-daily-full-run-builds-its-own-libraries"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-09 (audit of the 2026-11-08 landings): the daily bin/test --all on main (runs/merge-tests/<commit>-<uuid>) has been red three days running on seon.db-test/stored-values-compress-through-the-codecs-the-build-made, its first assertion: konserve.jolt.zstd/lib-path is the main tree's target/lib/libzstd.dylib, not under the run's own checkout. The run's checkout takes the main tree's libraries (bin/env's JOLT_ZSTD_LIB and JOLT_LZ4_LIB, from the environment it was started in) where bin/build would make its own. The test is right (a store's codecs are the ones the build made); the daily run's environment is wrong. Find where the run's checkout is made and started (seon.fx's :tests effect, the main-build slot) and make its bin/env name its own target/lib, as a worktree's does. Proof: the test green in the next daily run; no test changed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/stored-values-compress-through-the-codecs-the-build-made}}
 {:seon.task/name "main-past-its-last-full-run-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (2026-11-10): bin/test --all writes full-run.edn beside the verdict cache (the commit, :green or :red with each red, when), and bin/full-run says how many landings main is past it (bin/land before LANDED, bin/system status); the dashboard says nothing. The record stays a file, so a store erase keeps it (docs/merging.md#the-full-run-is-the-net). Plan: root's bridge (seon.system/landed-tx, bin/land's) writes on root's entity, with each landing, the landings since the last full run, its commit and its outcome, read from the file by bin/land and passed as data (root reads no file); a problem class shows it past :seon.config.proofs/full-run-landings (a new key: how many landings main may move past the net; provenance to be stated, the owner's to set) and when the last outcome is :red, naming the reds. No clock: a count of landings. Proof, red first: a landing past N with the last full run green is one row; a red last run is one row naming its reds; a landing within N with a green run is none."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/landing-count-past-full-run-landings-key-green}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-service-runs-each-item-once-for-every-asker"
  :seon.task/plan "The test system as a Flow (docs/merging.md#the-test-service; owner, 2026-11-10/11: \"I want the flow system that is always only letting the tests run that we have cores for\"). Built and on main by 2026-11-11: one queue of (test, digest) items for every asker (seon.prove/ask-items), each run once; admission by max-workers, max-cores and max-load (seon.prove/admission); an agent's turn first; process tests, check steps, builds and seeds as items on a run's checkout; HELD and RESUMED for the load; every asker hears every red it waits on (a turn its proofs, a tree's run every item). Left, the one piece no other task holds: a seon.cost proof that dispatch stays microseconds an item (grant scans the queue: O(queued) a grant; at 2,000 queued items that is the cost to bound). The pieces after it are their own tasks: seon.prove/the-shell-runner-is-deleted, seon.fx/process-tests-run-from-inside, seon.check/check-steps-are-tests-on-the-service, seon.prove/the-daily-full-run-is-a-service-run. Proof to write: seon.prove-cost-test/a-grant-costs-what-it-scans."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-digest-whichever-runner-plans-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11: one test on one program, seon.prove-test/a-worker-pulls-one-item-at-a-time, is digest 22c228440ccf3874 by the shell and 33dfdedd17e20795 by an agent of the standing system: the agent planned by empty inputs ({:tree \"\" :env \"\" :config {}}, seon.agent/test-inputs' fallback). Root hands agents the binary's inputs only when three tree ids agree (seon.system/booted-source: the binary's tree, bin/tree's with untracked files; the source fact's :tree, the catch-up's; target/seon.inputs' :tree), and a stray untracked file in the main checkout at build time (src/demo/math.clj, a write-back that reached the owner's working copy) made them differ, so every agent digested by nothing: no green shared with the shell (batch 82 through the system: 370 verdicts in 29 minutes of 1,600 items), and digests that omit pins and config. Design, ruled 2026-11-11 (the managing session's go; the owner may overrule): inputs stop depending on a tree id. A boot computes the inputs of the files its catch-up read (bin/system start, which can run git, hands them to root as it hands the port: seon.fx/inputs over the checkout as read), and root's :seon.system/source holds them; seon.agent/test-inputs reads them, and with none answers the typed :seon.agent/inputs-unknown, never an empty default (that lands with this, never before). Deleted: booted-source's tree equality, target/seon.inputs' tree keying, the test runner's write of it. Then plain bin/prove asks the system again (piece c), once a landing through it ends within a shell prove's time. Proofs to write: one tree planned by the shell and by an agent gives every test one digest; an agent whose root holds no inputs refuses to plan."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-no-asker-waits-on-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (batch 82): the managing session ended its landing's client, and the standing system went on running the tree's 1,370 queued items with no one waiting, until a restart. The service has no cancel, and a tree's :judge run waits on every item as its proof (every red to every asker), so an ended agent does not end it either; process and step items run under root's effect owner. Plan: an asker's connection that ends (the server's ask-streamed peer gone) withdraws it as a waiter (seon.prove/answer-asker's removal, which already drops a queued item no one waits on); a run with no waiter left ends, its running items' processes ended by their group (f2, bin/bounded); and a cancel by run key for a person. Proof to write: a run whose asker disconnects leaves no item queued."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-trees-check-store-is-seeded-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (batch 82): through the system, two check-step items each ran bin/check --steps in the tree, and both seeded its check-store: FileAlreadyExistsException on target/check-store, CHECK FAILED. The shell runner runs one bin/check --steps for every step, which seeds once. Plan: the tree's seed item ([:seed tree :stores], seon.prove/plan-items) seeds the check store too, and a step item never seeds (bin/check --steps given a seeded store). Proof to write: two step items of one tree granted at once both find the store seeded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proves-systems-end-with-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (Web; Data model earlier): readable's whole prove of 20:58 left 7 processes alive 3 h 16 m, three `seon root --profile scripted` and their bin/held drains (--detach --drain, one reparented to init), all under that tree's own target/runs/shard-* directory. A process test's root is started detached on purpose (it must outlive the test's request), so nothing ends it when the shard, or the prove, ends; bin/end-tree walks by parent and misses a detached group (the same class as the shard orphan Data model captured at 20:55). Plan: a shard's run directory is the key: at a shard's end, and again at the prove's end, every process whose command line names that run directory is ended by its process group after bin/evidence, and the prove prints any pid it could not end, never silence. Under the service the same holds per item (Optimization's bin/bounded ends an item's group; f2). Proof to write: a prove whose process test leaves a root running ends it, and its last lines name none left."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-shell-runner-is-deleted"
  :seon.task/plan "Piece e of the test system (docs/merging.md#the-test-service; owner, 2026-11-10: \"I don't want 'shards' anymore\"). What exists, 2026-11-11: plain bin/prove and bin/prove --merge (bin/land's) ask the standing system, which indexes the checkout into a branch (seon.system/prove-tree!) and proves it under its one admission (jolt/tests-sysprove). The shell runner stays only behind bin/prove --out, --tests and --checks, bin/check with no argument, the daily full run (seon.fx full run: bin/test --all) and the item bodies (bin/test --shard, bin/check --steps). Plan: once seon.fx/process-tests-run-from-inside and seon.check/check-steps-are-tests-on-the-service leave no item body calling it and the daily run is a service run (seon.prove/the-daily-full-run-is-a-service-run), delete test_runner's plan-of path and shards (write-shards!, worth-shards, memory-jobs, :seon.config.proofs/jobs), the proved marker (bin/prove's MARK), bin/stalls (its timer is bin/bounded's), and bin/prove's shell half. Proof: seon.main-test's whole-src check that nothing names bin/test --shard, red until the last caller goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-daily-full-run-is-a-service-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization (seon.fx). The daily full run (seon.prove full-request, :full mode) is a :tests effect that runs bin/test --all in a worktree of main (fx.clj, the full run's body): its own plan and shards, outside the service's admission. Plan: the full run is a request of the service like any other, its items every test and step of main at its digest with reuse off (:fresh? true items), so one admission covers it and its verdicts land as any run's do; seon.prove/full-answered's red-as-problem stays. Then the full run's shell body has no caller (seon.prove/the-shell-runner-is-deleted). Proof to write: a :full request's items are every key of main, none reused."
  :seon.task/kind :work
