 {:seon.task/name "a-test-binds-the-machine-keys-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 (the whole-load trial's control): run in an agent of the standing system, five seon.prove-test tests went red because the service's init read the admission in force, which there includes the main tree's config/local.edn (max-load 18.0), so a busy machine held their items: a verdict the machine's load could turn. Fixed in seon.prove-test (proc0: the shipped workers, no load bound) on jolt/tests-sysprove. The class: a test whose verdict reads a machine key (max-load, max-workers, max-cores, ports, cores, memory) through the config in force judges the machine it runs on. Plan: sweep the tests that reach seon.config/value of a :seon.config.machine/* or :seon.config.proofs/max-* key outside a binding of their own (the reach graph's config-reads names them) and give each its own value; then a law: a deftest's reach never reads a machine key unbound. Proof to write: the sweep's count is zero on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-queue-orders-by-work-not-wall-time"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Proposed 2026-11-11 (mail 1727, code read): seon.prove's grant orders queued items by item-value, (waiters + age) / ms, where ms is a test's measured WALL time (seon.fx/typical-ms, the median of its greens' :ms) or :seon.config.proofs/unmeasured-ms. Wall time is load: the same test measures 3x under a busy machine, so the order (never a verdict) moves with what else ran. Correct: order by work, as cost proofs measure it (docs/cost.md#what-is-measured): an in-process test's ticks (its verdict holds :ticks), a process item's or step's child CPU ms (rusage, which the runner can record beside :ms), falling back to the estimate only where none is recorded. Deletes the wall-ms input of item-value. Proof: two queued items whose wall ms disagree with their ticks are granted in tick order."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/the-queue-grants-by-recorded-work}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-gate-run-is-answered-when-every-item-has-its-verdict"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen on the standing store (Optimization, 2026-11-12, read at a REPL on main): the one task that reached the merge gate, seon.ctx/a-cites-part-shows-forms-not-templates, holds 242 deferred tests and 78 :seon.task/deferred-results (75 pass, 3 fail). Its results were written at 17:03:22Z, 7 s after a red verdict (seon.source-test/a-branchs-config-is-its-own-and-reaches-the-next-process at 17:03:15Z); of the 164 tests with no result, 94 have verdicts in the cache written from 17:00:58Z to 17:29:45Z, after the gate was answered, so the run was answered while its items still ran. By design (seon.prove/item-ended, 32e94c68b1 on main since 2026-11-11 14:26Z) a :judge run waits for every item past a red: ask-items gives its asker every item as a proof. Not yet known: whether the standing binary at that boot predated that commit, or whether the gate's run (seon.system/prove-tree! from seon.fx/run-tests, mode :judge, named tests) reaches its asker by another path whose :proofs is empty (item-ended then answers at the first red). First step: a test that asks a :judge run named by three process items, one red and two slower, and checks that the answer holds all three. The reconciler's side is done (jolt/redsat): a test the run answered nothing of is recorded :none and holds the task. See docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-judge-run-past-a-red-is-answered-with-every-item}}

 {:seon.task/name "one-grant-answers-differently-in-a-shard-and-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 (Tests, building seon.prove/batch-of): a rule that let a batch take more items while its measured ms was under :seon.config.proofs/child-min-ms granted two 5 ms items as ONE batch when called in an agent (load-file of the branch's seon.prove, with and without check-private!), and as TWO batches of one in a bin/test shard on the same tree (seon.prove-test, child-min-ms read as 100 in both). Rewritten as a plain loop, the shard still gave two. The rule was dropped from the tip, so the code is on no branch; the agent's run and the shard's are the evidence (Tests' transcript, 08:36-08:38). One function giving two answers is the finding: compiled (the shard's AOT) against interpreted (load-file), or a binding the shard holds that the agent does not. Proof, red first: the same call, ask-items with two items {:agent :a1 :ms 5} on two workers and the floor rule, answers the same batches in a bin/test shard and in an agent's eval. Owner: Tests, with Deep fixes if it is the compiler."
